1
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:07,733
Example A: the lecture's four examples and our 41-knob network.

2
00:00:08,333 --> 00:00:12,333
Four examples, three inputs each, and the answer wanted for each.

3
00:00:12,933 --> 00:00:17,733
The network starts with random weights, so it just guesses.

4
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:22,533
Its four guesses (blue) beside the four right answers (grey).

5
00:00:23,133 --> 00:00:27,667
Predict: must each blue bar move up or down to reach its target?

6
00:00:28,267 --> 00:00:33,133
2 must go up and 2 must go down: arrows point to the targets.

7
00:00:33,733 --> 00:00:48,600
One number for the total mistake: square each error, then add.

8
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,000
backward() gives each knob a gradient: its effect on the loss.

9
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:57,333
Predict: this gradient is negative. Knob up or down?

10
00:00:57,933 --> 00:01:14,933
Negative gradient: raising the knob lowers the loss, so go up.

11
00:01:15,533 --> 00:01:31,133
30 steps at 0.05. The lecture: 0.1, 20 steps, loss 0.0103.

12
00:01:31,733 --> 00:01:38,800
Loss fell from 6.2443 to 0.0346: blue bars meet grey.

13
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:51,400
Same run in the network: line thickness shows weight size.

14
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,267
Example B: what if the learning rate changes? Five runs, same start.

15
00:01:57,867 --> 00:02:02,067
Predict: all five start at the same loss. Which rate ends lowest?

16
00:02:02,667 --> 00:02:14,467
Each line is one run of 30 steps. We race them live.

17
00:02:15,067 --> 00:02:22,267
Fastest here is 0.3, not the biggest rate: 1.0 jumps and gets stuck.

18
00:02:22,867 --> 00:02:27,133
Why is 1.0 stuck? Compare its predictions with those of 0.3.

19
00:02:27,733 --> 00:02:32,733
Now the same network trained with rate 1.0: every bar jumps to -1.

20
00:02:33,333 --> 00:02:45,267
Two answers are now wrong, and tanh is flat there, so the gradient dies.

21
00:02:45,867 --> 00:02:49,933
Expert corner: what really goes wrong, and what real training adds.

22
00:02:50,533 --> 00:02:54,000
The classic bug: forget to zero the gradients before backward().

23
00:02:54,600 --> 00:03:05,467
Predict: if we never zero them, will the loss still go down?

24
00:03:06,067 --> 00:03:11,267
Yes, it still falls here, which is why the bug is so easy to miss.

25
00:03:11,867 --> 00:03:21,667
Kept gradients pile up, so every step is too big.

26
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:26,467
41 knobs but only 4 examples: the network can simply memorise them.

27
00:03:27,067 --> 00:03:35,467
Low loss on these four says nothing about unseen examples.

28
00:03:36,067 --> 00:03:41,600
The loss is a design choice: it decides what we aim for.

29
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:51,600
A mistake of 1.5 pulls with 3 under squared loss, 1 under absolute.

30
00:03:52,200 --> 00:04:08,133
Real training adds four things to this loop.

